WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.050
- Welcome, everyone to
another coffee break

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.960
here on my channel, Firm Learning.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.780
My name is Heinrich, I'm a
former McKinsey consultant,

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:09.890
and on my channel I talk
about how to become successful

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.130
in the first years of your career.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:16.220
And today, let's look into
what Steve Jobs had to say

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.390
about consulting.

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:20.900
So of course, if Steve says
something, we all better listen.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.230
Steve surely is a great
and intelligent man.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.460
And I found, I think, one
of the less known videos

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.440
of Steve Jobs, where he commented

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.170
on his perspective of consulting.

00:00:29.170 --> 00:00:31.160
The video is from a speech he gave at MIT

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.880
where he essentially shared
two points on consulting.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.713
So, let's look at them.

00:00:34.713 --> 00:00:36.720
I think part of what he says is correct,

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:38.070
but I do also think that his view

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:40.080
is at least partially flawed.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.100
So, let's get into it.

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.200
- How many of you are from
manufacturing companies?

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:45.680
Oh, excellent.

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:47.226
Where are the rest of you from?

00:00:47.226 --> 00:00:49.010
(audience laughing)

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:50.970
How many from consulting?

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.776
Oh, that's bad.

00:00:52.776 --> 00:00:54.910
(audience laughing)

00:00:54.910 --> 00:00:56.113
You should do something.

00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.560
No, seriously, I don't
think there's anything

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.810
inherently evil in consulting.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:04.353
I think that...

00:01:04.353 --> 00:01:07.260
(audience laughing)

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:11.280
I think that, without owning something

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:16.790
over an extended period
of time like a few years,

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:19.610
where one has a chance
to take responsibility

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.480
for one's recommendations;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.630
where one has to see one's recommendations

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.720
through all action stages,

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:28.000
and accumulate scar
tissue for the mistakes,

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:29.600
and pick oneself up off the ground

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:30.883
and dust oneself off,

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.223
one learns a fraction of what one can.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.690
Coming in and making recommendations

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.470
and not owning the results,

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:44.163
not owning the implementation,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.630
I think is a fraction of the value

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:54.623
and a fraction of the opportunity
to learn and get better.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.510
- So, here Steve Jobs
is essentially saying

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:01.250
that consultants do not own
the products of their work,

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.810
and do not follow through
with it in the execution.

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:04.820
While I think it's fair to say

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.130
that consultants of
course own the concepts

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.130
and strategies that they might develop,

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.160
especially MBB consulting;
so McKinsey, BCG, Bain,

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:14.800
surely it's correct that,
at least in most cases,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:17.270
you do not witness the execution

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.180
of these concepts and surely
also not responsible for them.

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:23.270
So, the one side of the
story is that he is correct.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:26.190
And I did also witness,
sometimes even up to a point

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.530
where I thought that this
was a little bit arrogant,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.660
that there was often a
little bit of the notion

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.500
that actually developing the
concepts and the strategies;

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.560
this is what the challenging part is.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.350
And then the execution that
wouldn't be that tough,

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:39.400
that difficult.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:40.650
And this is also the context

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.760
in which I released a
video a couple of weeks ago

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.730
about the Boston Consulting Group

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:47.050
and GameStop; legal dispute,

00:02:47.050 --> 00:02:49.510
where Boston Consulting
Group was suing GameStop

00:02:49.510 --> 00:02:52.340
for about $30 million in consulting fees.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.173
So, if you haven't seen that,

00:02:53.173 --> 00:02:54.820
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.350
But this video, this whole topic,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:58.530
would also very much linked to the fact

00:02:58.530 --> 00:02:59.560
that Boston Consulting Group

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.240
actually was not responsible,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:02.870
at least from their perspective,

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:04.560
for the actual implementation

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.820
of the measures that they recommended,

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:09.420
and there seem to be lots of
disputes surrounding that.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.030
So, this criticism that consultants

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:13.550
do not really own their
ideas from end to end,

00:03:13.550 --> 00:03:15.750
from very start where
you conceptualize them

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:17.700
until they're financially effective,

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.010
this is of course a common criticism.

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.580
Though here, I often wonder

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:23.980
whether that's really
a fair point to make,

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:27.010
because let's look at
other roles in corporate.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:29.370
Let's look at salespeople.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:32.030
Do they own everything of the value chain

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.770
of what happens in their company?

00:03:33.770 --> 00:03:35.360
No, they are just salespeople.

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:36.660
They're selling products.

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.360
And you could argue, this is
also only a very limited part

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:40.880
of what happens in a company.

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:42.120
What about accountants?

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:44.450
Financial accountants,
managerial accountants,

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.600
people working in a controlling team,

00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:50.660
do they really own the company
processes from end to end?

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.580
Well, I'd argue they're very efficient

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.920
and capable in a certain area.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.970
What if you are a software engineer?

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.910
So, here you certainly build something,

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:01.850
and are responsible for the
final product of your work,

00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:04.430
but here also, are you
really owning it end to end?

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.630
Someone else will usually conceptualize

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:10.040
and define the things that
then you are supposed to build.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.890
So, we get very detailed descriptions,

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:14.130
usually from the product
team telling you what to do,

00:04:14.130 --> 00:04:16.130
and then you do just that.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:18.080
Is this really end to end ownership?

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:20.270
Now, you could continue
talking about engineers

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.940
working in R&amp;D or people
working in production.

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:25.510
I'd say that in almost every role

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:28.370
in a corporate environment,
you are only responsible

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:31.610
for certain part of the
whole, but not everything.

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.200
And in this same way,
the strategy consultants

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.220
are responsible for developing
this strategy concepts,

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:38.540
and of course, many other things

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.200
depending on the types of
projects they're working on,

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:42.820
and they're just not that different

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:44.560
from many of these other roles as well.

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:49.230
- And so, you do get a
broad cut at companies,

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.120
but it's very thin.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.830
It's like a picture of a...

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.830
I'm a vegetarian, so I won't use steak,

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.443
but it's like a picture of a banana.

00:04:59.284 --> 00:05:00.430
(audience laughing)

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:03.010
You might get a very accurate picture,

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:05.260
but it's only two dimensional.

00:05:05.260 --> 00:05:09.860
And without the experience
of actually doing it,

00:05:09.860 --> 00:05:11.380
you never get three dimensional.

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:13.120
So, you might have a lot
of pictures on your walls,

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:14.350
you can show it off to your friends,

00:05:14.350 --> 00:05:15.900
You can say, "Look,
I've worked in bananas,

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.590
I've worked in peaches,
I've worked in grapes."

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.573
but you never really taste it.

00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:23.330
And I think that...

00:05:24.546 --> 00:05:25.654
that's what I think.

00:05:25.654 --> 00:05:29.370
(audience clapping)

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:30.880
- So, this point builds on the notion

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.950
that consultants don't really understand

00:05:32.950 --> 00:05:34.180
what they're talking about;

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:37.540
they're the best there for
very superficial knowledge

00:05:37.540 --> 00:05:39.840
of the things that they
express their opinions on.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.250
And here again, in favor of Steve Jobs,

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:43.860
of course, as a consultant

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:46.900
you will rarely have the
knowledge that someone has

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.990
who is really working
in an operational way

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:52.010
on a certain topic for
years and years and years.

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:54.880
Of course such a person will
likely be more knowledgeable,

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:57.610
at least on some parts of the
things than you as consultant.

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:59.370
And in addition to that,
I do think it's true

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.270
also from my personal experience,

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:02.830
that sometimes that's the problem;

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.940
that as a consultant, you need
to be perceived as competent,

00:06:05.940 --> 00:06:08.380
and you need to do things
to, to some extent,

00:06:08.380 --> 00:06:11.310
fake this competency, even
though you're still learning,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:13.260
you're still in the process of acquiring

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.380
all the knowledge that
you need to acquire.

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:17.410
There was a Harvard
business reviewer article

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.620
a couple of years ago

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:22.260
that called this the "learning
credibility tension".

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:23.980
That's a tension that many people

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:25.600
in professional services face.

00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.150
So, it's not only specific to consultant,

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.980
but a very relevant,
interesting topic indeed.

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.400
I created a video about
that a couple of months ago,

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.080
I will also link it above here,

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.910
with some very interesting
insights on that.

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:37.600
Check it out if this topic interests you.

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:39.910
But that being said, I
still think that this notion

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.180
that consultants don't really know

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:42.850
what they're talking about, in the end,

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:44.900
is still very much flawed as well.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:45.733
From my perspective,

00:06:45.733 --> 00:06:47.830
it's actually one of
the biggest advantages

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:49.220
of having worked several years

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:51.450
in consulting, that after this time,

00:06:51.450 --> 00:06:54.750
you will be able to have
a meaningful conversation

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:57.550
with any senior leader of most companies

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:00.310
independent from the
industry or from the function

00:07:00.310 --> 00:07:01.630
that the leader is working in,

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.070
just based on your insights

00:07:03.070 --> 00:07:05.600
and based on your contextual understanding

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.810
of what the key topics are that matter

00:07:07.810 --> 00:07:09.320
to these type of people,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:11.490
and of course also what they mean

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.390
on an executional operational level.

00:07:13.390 --> 00:07:14.223
Because in the end of the day,

00:07:14.223 --> 00:07:16.760
these are things that you will
at least have some exposure

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.920
on in your work as a consultant this way.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.610
On top of that, the big
variety of different topics

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:24.450
you will work on, at
least in MBB consulting,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:28.050
really enables you to, if
then you need to go deep

00:07:28.050 --> 00:07:29.510
in a certain part of your career

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.470
also after consulting, to
give you the confidence

00:07:32.470 --> 00:07:35.080
that you can understand
also complex things

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:36.540
in a short amount of time,

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.500
and give you the ability to really master

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:40.380
lots of knowledge in short time,

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:43.330
to get this done, and to
make yourself knowledgeable

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:44.590
whenever this is then needed.

00:07:44.590 --> 00:07:46.350
So, in the end of the day,
to me, this comes back

00:07:46.350 --> 00:07:49.820
to the old debate, whether
being a generalist is better,

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:52.060
or whether being a specialist is better.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.140
And in my humble opinion,

00:07:53.140 --> 00:07:54.890
there are very good arguments to be made

00:07:54.890 --> 00:07:57.600
that, especially in the
beginning of your career,

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:01.800
it makes a lot of sense to
become a generalist first.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.850
You will still have
plenty of opportunities

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:07.500
later down in your career
to amass niche knowledge,

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.440
to amass knowledge in
a very specific domain,

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.220
to become this very T-shaped profile.

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:14.840
Whereas on the one hand side,

00:08:14.840 --> 00:08:17.290
very broad general
understanding of things,

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.440
and then also is able to go deep

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:22.550
and has the main expertise
in some specific areas.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:23.730
That often makes a lot of sense,

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.400
because if you specialize yourself

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:27.200
too early on in your career,

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:28.470
but understand that the rewards

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:30.250
for that can be significant as well,

00:08:30.250 --> 00:08:31.820
then often it is difficult.

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:35.490
Then after years and years and
years in this specific role,

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:36.410
you then want to do things

00:08:36.410 --> 00:08:38.420
which are very very different from that.

00:08:38.420 --> 00:08:40.970
But I'm very interested to
hear your opinion as well.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.830
Do you actually agree with
what Steve Jobs said or not?

00:08:43.830 --> 00:08:46.070
Just leave a comment below
in the comment section.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.120
Very happy to have a
conversation with you.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:51.310
And as always, if you took any
value from this video at all,

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:54.010
destroy the Like button
for the YouTube algorithm,

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:55.920
and also subscribe to stay up to date.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:56.890
My name is Heinrich.

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:00.550
Big, big, thanks to the members
of the channel for support.

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:02.210
I release videos every single Saturday.

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.150
See you again next week.

00:09:03.150 --> 00:09:05.513
Until then, all the best
to you, and bye bye.

